>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `config`

``` hol4
Refute.type config

Refute.default_qc_config : qc_config
Refute.default_mf_config : mf_config
Refute.default_config : config
Refute.the_config : config ref
Refute.show_config : unit -> unit
Refute.apply_updates : config_update list -> config -> config
Refute.current_config : config_update list -> config
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Configures Refute searches.

A `config` is an immutable record.  Each updater takes a new field value
and maps the old configuration to the new one, so updaters compose with
`|>`.  `default_config` contains `default_qc_config` and
`default_mf_config`.  `the_config` is the mutable interactive default
read by `quickcheck`, `model_refute`, and the tactics when they are
applied; `show_config ()` prints its complete value.  `apply_updates
updates cfg` applies a list of updaters to `cfg` from left to right, and
`current_config updates` does the same to `!the_config`; `REFUTE_TAC_WITH`,
`refute_with`, and `refute_goal_with` are built from them.

``` hol4
val cfg = Refute.default_config
  |> Refute.upd_timeout 5.0
  |> Refute.upd_search
       (Refute.Only [Refute.Exhaustive, Refute.Narrowing])
  |> Refute.upd_size 7
```

### Common fields

``` hol4
Refute.upd_timeout : real -> config -> config
Refute.upd_search : search -> config -> config
Refute.upd_sequential : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_genuine_only : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_abort_potential : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_quiet : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_no_assms : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_evals : term list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_expect : expectation -> config -> config
Refute.upd_max_counterexamples : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_tag : string -> config -> config
Refute.upd_widths : int list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_qc : qc_config -> config -> config
Refute.upd_mf : mf_config -> config -> config
```

| Field | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `timeout` | `10.0` | Seconds in the whole-call search deadline. |
| `backends` | `NONE` | All backends, or exactly the names in `SOME`. |
| `sequential` | `false` | Run backends by weight instead of racing them. |
| `genuine_only` | `false` | Request only genuine hits. |
| `abort_potential` | `false` | Stop at the first hit of any certainty. |
| `quiet` | `false` | Suppress traces, messages, and warnings. |
| `no_assms` | `false` | Ignore assumptions passed to `refute_goal`. |
| `evals` | `[]` | Reconstruct and display these additional terms. |
| `expect` | `NoExpectation` | Check a regression expectation after the run. |
| `max_counterexamples` | `1` | Maximum number of QC hits requested. |
| `tag` | `""` | Append text to the formatted outcome. |
| `widths` | `[1,2,3,4]` | Widths tried for a word type variable. |

`timeout` is one absolute deadline for the whole call: preprocessing,
backend admission, compilation, testing, and solver calls all consume
the same remainder.  `backends` is set through `upd_search`, which takes
`AllBackends`, `QuickcheckBackends`, or `Only choices`; `Only []` is
rejected.  The expectations are `NoExpectation`, `ExpectNone`,
`ExpectUnknown`, `ExpectCex`, `ExpectModel`, `ExpectNoModel`,
`ExpectGenuine`, `ExpectQuasiGenuine`, and `ExpectPotential`; a mismatch
raises a HOL error.  A word type variable takes its instances from
`widths`, independently of the QC and MF sub-records.  `upd_qc` and
`upd_mf` replace a whole sub-record; the individual updaters are less
brittle.

### Quickcheck and narrowing fields

``` hol4
Refute.upd_size : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_iterative_size : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_iterations : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_depth : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_finite_types : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_finite_type_size : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_default_type : hol_type list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_instantiate :
  (hol_type option * hol_type) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_substrate : substrate_choice -> config -> config
Refute.upd_seed : int option -> config -> config
Refute.upd_allow_existentials : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_finite_functions : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_certify : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_smart_quantifier : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_smart_generators : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_optimise_equality : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_reorder_premises : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_use_subtype : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_allow_function_inversion : bool -> config -> config
```

| Field | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `size` | `10` | Maximum generated size and narrowing refinement depth. |
| `size_mode` | iterative | `upd_size` pins a fixed bound instead. |
| `iterations` | `100` | Number of random draws. |
| `depth` | `10` | Fuel supplied to each smart enumerator. |
| `finite_types` | `true` | Add small finite type instances. |
| `finite_type_size` | `3` | Number of finite cardinalities to consider. |
| `default_type` | `[``:num``]` | Instances if `finite_types` is false. |
| `instantiate` | `[]` | Per-type-variable pins overriding `finite_types`/`default_type`. |
| `substrate` | `Auto` | `Auto`, `NativeSML`, or `Compute`. |
| `seed` | `NONE` | Seed; `NONE` advances the session stream. |
| `allow_existentials` | `true` | Permit PNF existential narrowing. |
| `finite_functions` | `true` | Finitize narrowing function inputs. |
| `certify` | `true` | Independently certify QC and narrowing hits. |
| `smart_quantifier` | `true` | Compile premises into plans. |
| `smart_generators` | `true` | Enable Horn `Enum` plans. |
| `optimise_equality` | `true` | Bind suitable equality premises. |
| `reorder_premises` | `true` | Reorder premises during smart mode inference. |
| `use_subtype` | `false` | Test a generator-less typedef by transporting to its representation type. |
| `allow_function_inversion` | `false` | Invert an `f ... = res` premise via its graph clauses. |

The default `size_mode` is iterative deepening: `10` is an initial
window and testing deepens until the deadline.  `upd_size` pins a fixed,
reproducible bound; `upd_iterative_size` sets a different initial window
and restores deepening.  `depth` is the fuel given to each smart
enumerator, not a second search from zero to `depth`; narrowing does not
read it.  `allow_existentials` selects the PNF narrowing engine, and
`finite_functions` chooses update-chain or constant-function inputs
there.  `certify = false` skips QC evaluation and replay certification;
a hit stays `Genuine` with `cert = NONE`.  `allow_function_inversion`
is itself a smart-generator route, so it has no effect when
`smart_generators` is false.

`substrate` selects, it does not promise: under `Auto` a substrate that
declines the goal is skipped, whereas an explicitly selected substrate
that declines reports `Unknown`.  `Auto` tries `NativeSML` then
`Compute`.

By default QC substitutes one type for every type variable of a goal at
once.  `upd_instantiate` pins named type variables instead: a `SOME ty`
key pins that variable, and a `NONE` key is the fallback for every
non-width variable no `SOME` entry names, so a `NONE`-only row, like a
fully-`SOME` assignment, collapses the search to a single instance.  A
variable no entry reaches keeps taking the shared carrier or width, as
with `[]`.  A width variable (a word's index) takes a pin only from its
own `SOME` entry, and that pin must be a word-width type (`:1`, `:2`,
...), since carriers and widths are disjoint value spaces; otherwise it
keeps varying over `widths`.  Naming a type variable the goal lacks, a
width variable with a non-width type, or a pin value with type variables
of its own is a configuration error reported as `Unknown`.  Pinning
changes only which monomorphic proxies QC tests: a still-polymorphic
goal whose pinned instances are fully covered still reports `Unknown`,
never `NoCounterexample`.  The model finder is unaffected; its
equivalent is `upd_card [(SOME ty, [n])]`.

A free variable at a typedef type `t` (abstraction `abs`,
representation type `rty`, characteristic predicate `P`) usually leaves
QC with nothing to test.  `upd_use_subtype true` rewrites `!x : t. B x`
to `!r : rty. P r ==> B (abs r)`, contracting every `rep (abs r)` redex
back to `r`, and reports a hit's binding for `x` as `abs r`.  The guard
is the soundness condition: `abs` is a bijection only from `{r | P r}`,
and dropping it would let a representation value outside `t`'s image
manufacture a spurious refutation.  Exhaustion follows the ordinary QC
bookkeeping, but the generator ranges over all of `rty` without knowing
`P`, so a true transported goal reports `Unknown` unless its body
normalizes to a ground truth: transport aids counterexample search, not
verification.  A transported hit is `Genuine` but never certified, since
replaying the original closure would need the untransported `x`.
Transport is a last resort: a registered or abstract generator for `t`
wins, a `TypeBase` datatype wins, and the flag applies only to a
harvestable typedef with a real `TYPE_DEFINITION` theorem, which
excludes the model finder's synthetic frac and fmap typedef entries.  It
does not reach inside another type: a free variable of type `t list` is
untouched.  Deciding whether `t` is harvestable runs the model finder's
lazy harvest, so a QC-only call can populate the model finder's typedef
registry; the harvest is idempotent.  The model finder never sees the
rewrite, as it handles typedefs natively.  The default matches
Isabelle's `use_subtype`.

### Model-finder fields

``` hol4
Refute.upd_card :
  (hol_type option * int list) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_iterative_card :
  (hol_type option * int list) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_max : (term option * int list) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_mono :
  (hol_type option * bool option) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_wf :
  (term option * bool option) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_sat_solver : string -> config -> config
Refute.upd_batch_size : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_falsify : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_user_axioms : bool option -> config -> config
Refute.upd_destroy_constrs : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_total_consts : bool option -> config -> config
Refute.upd_peephole_optim : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_datatype_sym_break : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_kodkod_sym_break : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_max_potential : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_max_genuine : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_atoms :
  (hol_type option * string list) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_format :
  (term option * int list) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_show_types : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_show_skolems : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_show_consts : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_debug : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_overlord : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_max_threads : int -> config -> config
Refute.upd_tac_timeout : real -> config -> config
Refute.upd_specialize : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_box :
  (hol_type option * bool option) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_binary_ints : bool option -> config -> config
Refute.upd_bits : int list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_star_linear_preds : bool -> config -> config
Refute.upd_iter :
  (term option * int list) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_bisim_depth : int list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_finitize :
  (hol_type option * bool option) list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_whack : term list -> config -> config
Refute.upd_need : term list option -> config -> config
Refute.upd_merge_type_vars : bool -> config -> config
```

| Field | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `card` | fallback `[1,...,10]` | Finite cardinalities by type. |
| `card_mode` | iterative | `upd_card` pins fixed rows instead. |
| `max` | fallback `[~1]` | Constructor multiplicities; `~1` is automatic. |
| `mono` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced monotonicity by type. |
| `wf` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced well-foundedness by constant. |
| `sat_solver` | `"smart"` | Kodkodi SAT solver selection. |
| `batch_size` | `50` | Scope problems submitted per solver batch. |
| `falsify` | `true` | Falsify; false searches for a model. |
| `user_axioms` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced inclusion of user axioms. |
| `destroy_constrs` | `true` | Simplify constructor applications. |
| `total_consts` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced total encoding of constants. |
| `peephole_optim` | `true` | Enable relational peephole optimization. |
| `datatype_sym_break` | `5` | Datatype symmetry-breaking strength. |
| `kodkod_sym_break` | `15` | Kodkod symmetry-breaking limit. |
| `max_potential` | `1` | Global budget for potential models. |
| `max_genuine` | `1` | Global budget for genuine models. |
| `atoms` | fallback `[]` | Preferred printed atom names by type. |
| `format` | fallback `[1]` | Tuple curried arguments for display. |
| `show_types` | `false` | Include interpreted type universes. |
| `show_skolems` | `true` | Include Skolem values. |
| `show_consts` | `false` | Include reconstructed constant tables. |
| `debug` | `false` | Enable MF debug mode and one-problem batches. |
| `overlord` | `false` | Keep the solver's scratch files instead of deleting them. |
| `max_threads` | `0` | Kodkodi thread cap; zero leaves it unspecified. |
| `tac_timeout` | `0.5` | Seconds for inner MF tactics. |
| `specialize` | `true` | Specialize static higher-order arguments. |
| `box` | fallback `NONE` | Choose or force boxing. |
| `binary_ints` | `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly use binary integers. |
| `bits` | `[1,...,10]` | Candidate binary word widths. |
| `star_linear_preds` | `true` | Exact linear-predicate closure. |
| `iter` | fallback depth row | Fixpoint-unrolling depths. |
| `bisim_depth` | `[9]` | Codatatype depths; `~1` disables bisimulation. |
| `finitize` | fallback `NONE` | Choose or force finitization. |
| `whack` | `[]` | Definitions preprocessing should ignore. |
| `need` | `NONE` | Terms whose values must occur in a model. |
| `merge_type_vars` | `false` | Merge MF type-variable scopes. |

`card_mode` is likewise iterative by default: the fallback row is an
initial window that grows until the deadline.  `upd_card` pins fixed
rows and `upd_iterative_card` restores scanning; an iterative row must
be a consecutive prefix `[1, ..., n]`.

In row-valued fields a `NONE` key is the fallback and a `SOME` key an
override; Boolean row values use `NONE` for automatic choice and
`SOME b` to force it.  A `format` row groups consecutive curried
arguments into tuples for display: `[2]` tuples the final two arguments
and leaves earlier ones curried, and several integers describe
consecutive groups from left to right within the grouped suffix; the
fallback `[1]` changes nothing.  The default `iter` row is
`[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`; a mutual fixpoint group shares one row,
the first explicit row in cases-theorem order deciding it.
`merge_type_vars` shrinks the scope product but can lose models needing
different cardinalities for the merged variables.

Updaters validate the resulting record.  `widths` must be nonempty with
every entry between 1 and 32, and `bits` nonempty with every entry
between 1 and 31.  `default_type` must be nonempty; it supplies every
instance when `finite_types` is false.  Every `iter` row must be nonempty and nonnegative
with a representable successor; a `bisim_depth` row must not mix `~1`
with nonnegative depths, which likewise need a representable successor.
Non-fallback keys of `max`, `wf`, and `iter` must be constants or
variables, and of `instantiate` type variables.  `max_genuine` must be
positive and `max_potential` nonnegative.

